@database "llm_openai_compat"
@author "Enrico Ehrich"
@$VER: llm_openai_compat.guide 1.0 (30.09.2026)
@smartwrap

@node main "OpenAI-compatible LLM providers"
@{h1}OpenAI-compatible LLM providers

This plugin lets agents talk to every endpoint that speaks the OpenAI wire
format -- OpenRouter, api.openai.com, DeepSeek, a local LM Studio, vLLM or
llama.cpp server -- over plain httpx, with no vendor SDK. It is an LLM
provider plugin: it has no tools, no hooks and no panel. You use it by
naming one of its providers in a model entry of @{tt}config/llm.yaml@{utt}
(or @{tt}config/llm_openrouter.yaml@{utt}); agents reach that entry through
a profile.

@{table}
Provider name | Kind | Endpoint
openai_httpx | chat | POST <base_url>/chat/completions
openai_responses | chat | POST <base_url>/responses
openai_httpx | batch | the OpenAI Batch API at api.openai.com
openai_speech | text to speech | POST <base_url>/audio/speech
@{body}

How models, profiles and fallback chains work in general is in
@{" LLM models and profiles " link scarabhive/llm}; this manual covers what
these providers do with an entry.

@{code}
For users (the operator writing llm.yaml)
  @{"  Which route to take  " link routes}
  @{"  Model entry keys  " link keys}
  @{"  API keys and endpoints  " link apikeys}
  @{"  Timeouts and streaming  " link timeouts}
  @{"  Retries and self-healing  " link retries}
  @{"  Reasoning  " link reasoning}
  @{"  Prompt caching  " link caching}
  @{"  OpenRouter routing  " link openrouter}
  @{"  Usage, cost and the hooks  " link usage}
  @{"  Errors  " link errors}
  @{"  Batch and speech  " link batch}

For agents and developers
  @{"  What the model sees  " link model}
  @{"  Setting it up  " link setup}
@{body}
@endnode

@node routes "Which route to take"
@{h1}Which route to take

@{b}openai_responses@{ub} speaks the Responses API. A reasoning model's
output items (reasoning with its encrypted content, function calls, the
message) are stored on the assistant message and sent back @{b}verbatim@{ub}
on the next turn. Take it for OpenAI reasoning models (GPT-5.x, o-series)
and for Gemini behind OpenRouter: on Chat Completions the gateway has to
rebuild those items from a flattened message, and that translation is where
the "encrypted content could not be verified" 400s come from. Its default
endpoint is OpenRouter.

@{b}openai_httpx@{ub} speaks Chat Completions. Take it for everything that
is not an OpenAI-style reasoning chain: DeepSeek, local servers, and Claude
behind OpenRouter -- OpenRouter forwards Claude's @{tt}cache_control@{utt}
markers only on Chat Completions (see @{" Prompt caching " link caching}).
Its default endpoint is api.openai.com.

@{table}
Difference | openai_httpx | openai_responses
default base_url | https://api.openai.com/v1 | https://openrouter.ai/api/v1
reasoning_details_mode default | keep_last | keep_all
thinking_budget | sent as reasoning.max_tokens when no thinking_level is set | ignored, with a warning
max_tokens is sent as | max_tokens | max_output_tokens
safety_identifier | not sent | sent
OpenRouter app name (X-Title, HTTP-Referer per agent) | sent | not sent
retries (see below) | 1 retry on 5xx and transport errors, 2 backed-off 429 retries | 2 retries on 5xx and transport errors, none on a plain 429
stored on the assistant message | reasoning_details as the gateway sent them | one block with all output items
@{body}

Switching an agent between the routes mid-session is safe: each route
ignores the other's stored reasoning and starts a fresh chain.
@endnode

@node keys "Model entry keys"
@{h1}Model entry keys

The keys of a model entry these providers read. Everything else in
@{tt}LLMModelConfig@{utt} is either handled by the core (@{tt}extends@{utt},
profiles) or not read here. A key that belongs to another provider's dialect
(@{tt}thinking_request_shape@{utt}, and @{tt}assistant_reasoning_field@{utt}
on openai_responses) is logged as "not wired ... will be ignored" when the
client is built.

@{table}
Key | Route | Default | What the client does with it
provider | both | -- | openai_httpx or openai_responses
model | both | -- | the model id sent to the endpoint
api_key | both | from the endpoint's variable | see @{" API keys and endpoints " link apikeys}
base_url | both | route default above | the endpoint; its host decides the key and whether OpenRouter extras are sent
context_window | both | httpx: the model-config default 32768; Responses: that, or 200000 if 0 | only reported to the core, never sent
max_tokens | both | none | output cap; 0 or unset sends nothing
temperature | both | none | sent only when no reasoning field is sent (httpx: neither thinking_level nor thinking_budget; Responses: no thinking_level)
thinking_level | both | none | reasoning.effort (none, minimal, low, medium, high, xhigh, max)
thinking_budget | httpx | none | reasoning.max_tokens, only without thinking_level
include_thoughts | -- | -- | no request field on either route (httpx warns)
parallel_tool_calls | both | true | sent as given; null leaves the field out
service_tier | both | none | sent as given (flex, priority, ...); dropped for the retry after a rate limit in the body (openai_httpx) or any 429 (openai_responses)
capabilities | both | all defaults | streaming decides the path; image_input, audio_input, video_input gate media; structured_output allows response formats; developer_role; default_api_type must be chat_completions on openai_httpx
safety_settings | both | none | list of {category, threshold}, sent whenever set
tool_schema_dialect | both | json_schema | gemini_function_declarations strips what Gemini's function declarations reject
assistant_reasoning_field | httpx | omit | reasoning_content: every assistant message carries its thinking back (DeepSeek thinking mode needs it on tool turns)
reasoning_details_mode | both | httpx keep_last, Responses keep_all | how many stored reasoning blocks travel back (see @{" Reasoning " link reasoning})
prompt_cache_key, prompt_cache_mode, prompt_cache_marker_style, prompt_cache_options | both | none | see @{" Prompt caching " link caching}
provider_routing, provider_affinity_minutes, plugins | both | none; affinity 30 min | OpenRouter only, see @{" OpenRouter routing " link openrouter}
safety_identifier | Responses | none | sent as is
request_timeout, httpx_timeouts, stream_silence_timeout | both | see @{" Timeouts and streaming " link timeouts} |
@{body}

An unknown value for tool_schema_dialect, assistant_reasoning_field,
reasoning_details_mode or prompt_cache_marker_style is refused when the
client is built -- a typo never quietly buys another dialect's default.

@{code yaml}
llm_system:
  models:
    deepseek-pro:
      provider: openai_httpx
      model: deepseek-v4-pro
      api_key: ${DEEPSEEK_API_KEY}
      base_url: https://api.deepseek.com/v1
      assistant_reasoning_field: reasoning_content
      context_window: 1000000
    or-gpt:
      provider: openai_responses
      model: openai/gpt-5.4
      api_key: ${OPENROUTER_API_KEY}
      thinking_level: medium
      stream_silence_timeout: 900
      prompt_cache_key: auto
@{body}
@endnode

@node apikeys "API keys and endpoints"
@{h1}API keys and endpoints

An @{tt}api_key@{utt} in the entry always wins. Without one, the key
follows the @{b}endpoint@{ub} the entry talks to, never the provider name:

@{table}
Host of base_url | Key taken from
openrouter.ai (and its subdomains) | OPENROUTER_API_KEY
api.openai.com | OPENAI_API_KEY
api.typesafe.ai | TYPESAFE_API_KEY
a local host (see @{"llm_common" link "llm_common/apikeys"}) | OPENAI_API_KEY
anything else | nothing: building the client fails
@{body}

The failure names the host and the variable; set @{tt}api_key:
${YOUR_VARIABLE}@{utt} on the entry. A missing gateway key therefore never
sends the OpenAI key to a third party. Note that @{tt}${VAR}@{utt} of an
unset variable expands to an empty string, which counts as "no key", as
does a key of only whitespace.

The batch backend is the exception: it always talks to api.openai.com with
the entry's @{tt}api_key@{utt} or OPENAI_API_KEY, whatever base_url says.

TLS verification follows the app's setting.
@endnode

@node timeouts "Timeouts and streaming"
@{h1}Timeouts and streaming

@{h2}Streaming or not

A model streams unless its entry says @{tt}capabilities.streaming:
false@{utt}. Streaming is what lets the web UI show text and thinking as it
arrives, and it changes what the read timeout means. With it off, the client
waits for the whole answer in one response.

@{h2}The four timeouts

Each client gets connect, read, write and pool timeouts. A key set in the
entry's @{tt}httpx_timeouts@{utt} wins; a key it does not set falls back:

@{table}
Timeout | openai_httpx | openai_responses
connect | 10 s | 10 s
read | request_timeout if the entry sets it, else 180 s | request_timeout if the entry sets it, else 600 s
write | 10 s | 30 s
pool | 5 s | 5 s
@{body}

An entry without its own @{tt}httpx_timeouts@{utt} gets the system default
@{tt}llm_system.httpx_timeouts@{utt} (the shipped @{tt}config/llm.yaml@{utt}
sets all four, read 180 s) -- except its @{tt}read@{utt} when the entry sets
@{tt}request_timeout@{utt}: an explicit per-model @{tt}request_timeout@{utt}
wins over the system read timeout. A @{tt}read@{utt} in the entry's own
@{tt}httpx_timeouts@{utt} wins over both.

@{h2}What read means

@{bullet}@{b}openai_httpx, streaming:@{ub} the longest gap between two pieces
of the stream. A gap longer than that is a stalled stream and is retried
like a dropped connection. The connection also sends TCP keep-alives (on
Linux after 15 s idle, every 15 s, 5 probes), so a long thinking pause is not
cut by a firewall.
@{bullet}@{b}openai_responses, streaming:@{ub} the socket's read timeout,
the same thing in practice.
@{bullet}@{b}Not streaming:@{ub} the longest silence while the endpoint
generates the whole answer -- a long answer needs a long read timeout.

@{h2}stream_silence_timeout

OpenRouter keeps a stream alive with comment lines about twice a second,
which reset the read timeout: an upstream that stopped answering would hold
the call for as long as the gateway keeps it open. With
@{tt}stream_silence_timeout@{utt} set, a stream that sends nothing but such
comments for that many seconds is given up and retried. Unset, there is no
limit of ours. Every real event counts as progress, thinking and usage-only
chunks included.

@{h2}How a stream ends

@{bullet}An answer whose finish reason has arrived is kept, however the
stream ends after it (missing @{tt}[DONE]@{utt}, stall, dropped connection).
@{bullet}openai_httpx: a stream that ends without @{tt}[DONE]@{utt} and
without a finish reason is handed over as it is, with finish reason
@{tt}incomplete_stream@{utt}.
@{bullet}openai_responses: a stream without a terminal event
(@{tt}response.completed@{utt}, @{tt}.incomplete@{utt} or
@{tt}.failed@{utt}) is retried; when the retries are spent, the text that
arrived is handed over with finish reason @{tt}incomplete_stream@{utt}.
@{bullet}A run cancelled while a piece of the stream is being handed out
stops before the client waits for the next one; one cancelled while the
client waits stops when the next bytes arrive (a keep-alive comment is
enough). The cancellation reaches the agent as a cancellation, never as an
answer.
@endnode

@node retries "Retries and self-healing"
@{h1}Retries and self-healing

Every retry is reported to the post_llm_response hooks as its own row
(@{tt}[RETRY n/m] ...@{utt}, finish reason @{tt}retry@{utt}) and as a status
line in the chat. A cancelled run interrupts every wait. A retry starts from
scratch: when the caller has already received deltas of the failed attempt,
the stream first sends one @{tt}{"type": "stream_restart"}@{utt} chunk, and
the caller drops what it has shown of that attempt.

@{h2}openai_httpx

Up to three attempts in all, shared by every kind of retry below.

@{table}
What happened | What the client does
HTTP 429 | up to 2 retries; wait: Retry-After seconds, else 60 s x 1.5^attempt, plus 0-50 % jitter; no wait when an OpenRouter pin was released. Then LLMQuotaExhaustedError if the body says "quota" or "exhausted", else LLMRateLimitError
HTTP 5xx | 1 retry after 1 s (none if a pin was released), then LLMServerError
any other error chunk, or finish reason @{tt}error@{utt}, in a stream | not an answer: retried like a dropped stream, from scratch, then LLMConnectionError
429 inside an HTTP 200 body (error code 429 or rate-limit wording) | same schedule as a 429, and the retry drops service_tier (flex to standard). Streaming: then LLMRateLimitError; not streaming: an answer carrying upstream_error_429
connect/read timeout, dropped or stalled stream, network error | 1 retry after 1 s, then LLMConnectionError
404 under an OpenRouter pin | pin released, retried at once
400 "encrypted content ... rs_..." (status or body) | two healing retries: drop the named reasoning item, then all reasoning_details -- in the request AND in the session, so the next turn does not hit it again
400 "thought signature" (status), or any 400 in a body, with Gemini signatures in the request | one retry with Google's bypass token in place of each signature
Gemini MALFORMED_FUNCTION_CALL without tool calls, or a function call leaked as text (not streaming) | 1 retry after 1 s
@{body}

@{h2}openai_responses

Up to three attempts; some retries below cost no attempt, because the
request changes.

@{table}
What happened | What the client does
connect/read timeout, network error | 2 retries after 2 s and 4 s, then the httpx error itself
HTTP 5xx | 2 retries after 2 s and 4 s (none if a pin was released), then LLMServerError
HTTP 429 | once: drop service_tier and retry (no attempt used); once: release an OpenRouter pin and retry (no attempt used); then at once LLMQuotaExhaustedError / LLMRateLimitError -- no backed-off 429 retries on this route
404 under an OpenRouter pin | pin released, retried (no attempt used)
400 or 404 rejecting replayed reasoning (encrypted content, thought signature, "produced under a different model") | once: all reasoning artifacts stripped from the session, request rebuilt (no attempt used)
stream without a terminal event | 2 retries after 2 s, then the partial text (see timeouts)
body that is no JSON | 2 retries after 2 s, then the JSON error
rate limit inside a 200 body | drop service_tier once (no attempt used), then retries with 2 s, 4 s
server_error inside a 200 body | 2 retries after 2 s, 4 s
any other error inside a 200 body | handed to the agent as an answer carrying the error (see errors)
@{body}

The agent server's own fallback chain (@{tt}llm_profile@{utt} with more than
one profile) takes over where these retries end; see
@{" Writing an agent " link scarabhive/agentdev}.
@endnode

@node reasoning "Reasoning"
@{h1}Reasoning

@{h2}Asking for it

@{tt}thinking_level@{utt} is sent as @{tt}reasoning: {effort: <level>}@{utt}
on both routes. On openai_httpx, @{tt}thinking_budget@{utt} is sent as
@{tt}reasoning: {max_tokens: <n>}@{utt} -- only without a level, because
OpenRouter takes one or the other. Without either, no reasoning field is sent
and the model's default applies. @{tt}temperature@{utt} is left out whenever
a reasoning field is sent, @{tt}none@{utt} included: OpenAI's hybrid models
refuse it.

@{h2}Watching it

Streamed thinking reaches the chat live: Chat Completions deltas named
@{tt}reasoning_content@{utt} (DeepSeek) or @{tt}reasoning@{utt} (OpenRouter),
Responses events @{tt}response.reasoning_text.delta@{utt} and
@{tt}response.reasoning_summary_text.delta@{utt} (OpenAI models only send the
summary). The text is stored once: inside the reasoning blocks where the
provider sent it there, else as @{tt}reasoning_content@{utt} on the message.

@{h2}Sending it back

Reasoning blocks (Gemini thought signatures, OpenAI encrypted items) must
travel back on later turns, or the provider refuses the turn.
@{tt}reasoning_details_mode@{utt} decides how many:

@{table}
Mode | Blocks sent back | For
keep_last | the latest assistant message's only | Gemini on Chat Completions (a signature is checked for the current turn)
keep_all | every assistant message's | encrypted chains checked across turns, and every model whose prompt cache matches byte for byte: stripping older blocks rewrites the prefix each turn
strip | none | endpoints that refuse them
@{body}

When a compaction or summary rewrote the history, the chain before it is
gone: openai_responses rebuilds such a message from its text and tool calls
(a fresh chain), openai_httpx with keep_all drops its blocks -- except while
that turn's tool calls are still open.

@{tt}assistant_reasoning_field: reasoning_content@{utt} (openai_httpx) puts
the thinking text on every assistant message, an empty string where there is
none. DeepSeek's thinking mode answers 400 without it on a tool round trip.

@{h2}Usage

Reasoning tokens are reported as the provider counts them, as
@{tt}completion_tokens_details.reasoning_tokens@{utt} -- a detail of the
completion, never computed from it. Some providers report more reasoning
tokens than completion tokens; that is what they sent, not an error here.
@endnode

@node caching "Prompt caching"
@{h1}Prompt caching

How the core splits a prompt into cacheable blocks is described in
@{" LLM models and profiles " link scarabhive/llm}. What these routes send:

@{table}
Key | Effect
prompt_cache_key | sent as @{tt}prompt_cache_key@{utt}; on OpenRouter also as @{tt}session_id@{utt}, the gateway's sticky-routing key (its cache lives on one backend). @{tt}auto@{utt} derives the key from the stable head of the conversation, so parallel runs with different prompts do not share one
prompt_cache_marker_style | which markers go into the prompt at the core's block boundaries: @{tt}openai@{utt} (prompt_cache_breakpoint parts), @{tt}anthropic@{utt} (cache_control), @{tt}none@{utt} (boundaries removed)
prompt_cache_mode | which boundaries get markers (auto, multi_turn, task_sequence, one_shot, off)
prompt_cache_options | passed through verbatim, e.g. {mode: explicit} so only our breakpoints cache
@{body}

@{h2}Marker style per route

@{bullet}@{b}openai_httpx@{ub}: unset means @{tt}openai@{utt} when a
prompt_cache_key is set, else @{tt}none@{utt}. OpenAI markers are placed only
with a key. @{tt}anthropic@{utt} marks the last system message, the last
tool, and -- when the mode caches the conversation -- the conversation's
tail, never more than Anthropic's four blocks. This is the route for Claude
behind OpenRouter.
@{bullet}@{b}openai_responses@{ub}: unset means @{tt}openai@{utt}; markers
are placed only with a prompt_cache_key. @{tt}anthropic@{utt} becomes one
top-level @{tt}cache_control@{utt} (OpenRouter forwards nothing else on this
route), which caches the whole conversation and only when the mode caches
the conversation.

@{h2}Keeping the prefix stable

@{bullet}A run note that changes on every call is never given a breakpoint;
developer notes are lowered to their role only after the markers are placed.
@{bullet}With @{tt}keep_last@{utt}, older reasoning blocks disappear from
the history turn by turn, which rewrites the prefix; @{tt}keep_all@{utt}
keeps it byte-stable.
@{bullet}On OpenRouter, a call pinned to the backend that answered the run
before reads that backend's cache (see
@{" OpenRouter routing " link openrouter}).
@endnode

@node openrouter "OpenRouter routing"
@{h1}OpenRouter routing

These extras are sent only when the entry's base_url is on openrouter.ai.

@{bullet}@{tt}X-OpenRouter-Metadata: enabled@{utt} on every request, so the
answer names the backend that served it. That name is stored on the
assistant message (@{tt}served_by@{utt}) and reported to the hooks.
@{bullet}@{tt}provider_routing@{utt} is sent as the request's
@{tt}provider@{utt} object (order, allow_fallbacks, sort, ...);
@{tt}llm_system.openrouter_routing@{utt} is merged in by the core, the entry
winning per key.
@{bullet}@{b}The pin.@{ub} A request goes to the backend that answered the
run's latest turn, or -- for a run without one -- the backend that answered
this agent on this model within @{tt}provider_affinity_minutes@{utt}
(default 30, 0 turns this off), as @{tt}order: [that one],
allow_fallbacks: false@{utt}. That backend holds the prompt cache and is the
only one that can verify the reasoning the run replays. A configured
@{tt}order@{utt} that does not list the backend is left alone. When the
pinned backend refuses (a 429, a 5xx that is retried, a 404, a rate limit
or server error in the body), the pin is released and the retry goes out as configured. The
gateway's backend names are translated to routing slugs from its
@{tt}/providers@{utt} list, reloaded at most every ten minutes; until it
loads, nothing is pinned.
@{bullet}@{tt}plugins@{utt} (context-compression, response-healing, ...)
are passed through verbatim, and only here -- on another endpoint they are
not sent, with a warning when the client is built.
@{bullet}openai_httpx also names the agent to OpenRouter (@{tt}X-Title@{utt}
and an @{tt}HTTP-Referer@{utt} ending in the agent's name), so each agent is
a row of its own in OpenRouter's activity list.
@endnode

@node usage "Usage, cost and the hooks"
@{h1}Usage, cost and the hooks

Each request is reported to the @{tt}pre_llm_request@{utt} hooks before it
is sent, and to the @{tt}post_llm_response@{utt} hooks when it ends. The
message debugger, the OpenTelemetry chat span and the cost figures are built
from these reports (@{" Hooks " link scarabhive/hooks},
@{" The capture hooks " link message_debugger/hooks},
@{" What gets exported " link otel/spans},
@{" What it records " link context_usage_tracker/hooks}).

@{h2}One report per ending

@{table}
The request ended with | post_llm_response carries
an answer | usage, finish reason, served_by; response_data when not streamed
a retry | an error @{tt}[RETRY n/m] ...@{utt}, finish reason retry, no usage
a refusal after the retries | the error (@{tt}HTTP 400: ...@{utt}, @{tt}Rate limit: ...@{utt}, @{tt}Request timed out: ...@{utt}, @{tt}Network/protocol error: Upstream error in stream: ...@{utt}, ...), no usage
a cancellation while the request was out | the error @{tt}Request cancelled during streaming@{utt} (or @{tt}cancelled@{utt}); on openai_httpx the usage too, when its chunk had arrived
a cancellation during a retry wait | the error @{tt}Request cancelled during retry wait@{utt}, after the retry's row
a cancellation before it was sent | the error @{tt}Request cancelled by user@{utt} (openai_httpx)
a stream the caller stopped reading | the error @{tt}stream abandoned by the caller@{utt}
anything unexpected | the exception's text
@{body}

A cancelled stream is billed for what the provider generated, but its usage
usually comes last, so the report mostly has none: the tokens of a cancelled
call are missing from the figures. On openai_responses every attempt is
reported as a request of its own, so each attempt has a pair of rows.

@{h2}The usage

@{bullet}Chat Completions: the provider's @{tt}usage@{utt} as it came,
@{tt}prompt_tokens@{utt} including cached tokens,
@{tt}prompt_tokens_details.cached_tokens@{utt} as the subset. Streams ask
for it with @{tt}stream_options.include_usage@{utt}.
@{bullet}Responses: mapped to the same shape -- input_tokens to
prompt_tokens, output_tokens to completion_tokens, the cached and reasoning
details as subsets; everything else (OpenRouter's @{tt}cost@{utt},
@{tt}cost_details@{utt}, @{tt}is_byok@{utt}) passed through.
@{bullet}Cost: where the provider reports its own @{tt}cost@{utt}
(OpenRouter) that figure wins; otherwise the core prices the tokens from
@{tt}config/llm_pricing.yaml@{utt}.
@endnode

@node errors "Errors"
@{h1}Errors

What reaches the agent server when a request cannot be answered. The server
decides from the type whether to fall back to the next profile of the chain
(@{" Writing an agent " link scarabhive/agentdev}).

@{table}
Raised | When
LLMRateLimitError | 429 after the retries (openai_httpx), 429 after at most a tier drop and a pin release (openai_responses), a streamed body-level 429 after the retries
LLMQuotaExhaustedError | the same, with "quota" or "exhausted" in the body
LLMServerError | 5xx after the retries
httpx.HTTPStatusError | any other 4xx, streamed or not; the message is @{tt}HTTP <status>: <body start>@{utt}. Also (status 400) a streamed body-level 400 that no healing applies to: @{tt}Unrecoverable body-400 in stream: ...@{utt}
LLMConnectionError | openai_httpx: timeout, stall, network error or an upstream error mid-stream, after the retries
httpx.TimeoutException / TransportError | openai_responses: the same, untyped
asyncio.CancelledError | the run was cancelled
ValueError at build time | no key for the endpoint; an unknown dialect value
NotImplementedError at build time | openai_httpx with capabilities.default_api_type other than chat_completions
@{body}

@{h2}Errors that come as an answer

Some failures arrive as an answer with an empty text and an
@{tt}error@{utt} on the assistant message, which the agent server treats as
an upstream failure of that model:

@{bullet}an error inside an HTTP 200 body that no retry covers (not
streamed, or on openai_responses): @{tt}upstream_error_<code>@{utt};
@{bullet}a content filter without tool calls (openai_httpx):
@{tt}content_filter_<native reason>@{utt};
@{bullet}a response openai_httpx cannot parse:
@{tt}response_format_error@{utt}.

An answer cut off by its output cap has finish reason @{tt}length@{utt}
(Responses: @{tt}max_output_tokens@{utt} and a missing reason both map to
it), a filtered Responses answer @{tt}content_filter@{utt}.
@endnode

@node batch "Batch and speech"
@{h1}Batch and speech

@{h2}Batch

A model entry with @{tt}provider: batch@{utt} and @{tt}batch_provider:
openai_httpx@{utt} runs its requests through the OpenAI Batch API: a JSONL
file uploaded to @{tt}/files@{utt}, a batch on
@{tt}/v1/chat/completions@{utt} with a 24-hour window, results downloaded
when it is done. The batch always goes to api.openai.com -- a gateway has no
batch API, and base_url is not used. Each request carries model, messages,
max_tokens and tools, nothing else: no reasoning level, no temperature.
Network errors while uploading the input or downloading the results are
retried three times (1 s doubling, at most 30 s). The queue and its settings (@{tt}llm_system.batch.providers.openai_httpx@{utt})
belong to the core; see @{" Batch Monitor " link batch_monitor/main}.

@{h2}Speech

@{tt}provider: openai_speech@{utt} in @{tt}tts_models@{utt} synthesises with
@{tt}POST <base_url>/audio/speech@{utt} -- OpenAI's API, which OpenRouter
offers for every speech model behind it.

@{table}
Key | Default | Meaning
model | -- | the speech model
base_url | https://openrouter.ai/api/v1 | the endpoint; the key follows it as above
api_key | from the endpoint's variable |
voice | none | the voice when the caller names none; a call without either fails
request_timeout | 300 | seconds per attempt
max_retries | 3 | retries on 429, 500, 502-504, 520-524, 529 and transport errors, after 2 s, 4 s, 6 s ...
@{body}

@{bullet}Audio comes back as raw PCM, 16 bit mono; the sample rate is read
from the Content-Type, else 24000.
@{bullet}A voice with a reference sample is cloned: the sample (at most
15 MiB; WAV, FLAC, OGG, AIFF, MP4/M4A or MP3) and its transcript travel in
every request as @{tt}input_references@{utt}, and no voice name is sent.
OpenRouter answers 404 for a model that cannot clone.
@{bullet}A style prompt, language or seed has no field on this API; the
client warns once and ignores them.
@{bullet}No usage is reported: the call appears in the message debugger,
not in the token and cost figures.
@endnode

@node model "What the model sees"
@{h1}What the model sees

@{h2}Messages

@{bullet}Only the fields the API knows are sent; internal bookkeeping fields
are removed. On openai_httpx, tool calls keep id, type and function (and
Gemini's @{tt}extra_content@{utt}); openai_responses replays its own turns as
the stored items and rebuilds other turns from text and tool calls.
@{bullet}Developer notes: openai_httpx sends them as @{tt}system@{utt} unless
the entry's @{tt}capabilities.developer_role@{utt} allows
@{tt}developer@{utt} (llama.cpp renders a role it does not know as nothing);
openai_responses keeps @{tt}developer@{utt} unless the entry lowers it. A
note that ends the request goes as a user message wrapped in note tags,
whatever the rung -- a request that ends on something needing no answer gets
none.
@{bullet}A role openai_responses does not know is sent as a user turn, with
a warning.

@{h2}Media

@{bullet}openai_httpx sends audio and video parts only when the entry
declares @{tt}audio_input@{utt} / @{tt}video_input@{utt}.
@{bullet}openai_responses replaces an image without @{tt}image_input@{utt},
audio without @{tt}audio_input@{utt}, and any video (the Responses API has
no video item) by a visible note such as
@{tt}[image input omitted: model has no image_input capability]@{utt}, and
logs a warning.
@{bullet}Images a tool returns follow the tool result as a user message, on
both routes.

@{h2}Tools

Tool schemas go out as the agent built them; with
@{tt}tool_schema_dialect: gemini_function_declarations@{utt} the keywords
Gemini refuses (additionalProperties, default, format, title, anyOf, oneOf,
...) are removed first. @{tt}tool_choice@{utt} is @{tt}auto@{utt}. On
openai_responses every tool carries @{tt}parameters@{utt}, @{tt}{}@{utt} for
none.

@{h2}Structured output

A response format is sent as @{tt}response_format@{utt} (Chat Completions)
or @{tt}text.format@{utt} (Responses) when the entry declares
@{tt}capabilities.structured_output@{utt}; a client asked for a format it
cannot send refuses the call rather than return free text.
@endnode

@node setup "Setting it up"
@{h1}Setting it up

Nothing to enable: the core finds LLM provider plugins by their
@{tt}plugin.toml@{utt} (@{tt}type = ["llm-provider"]@{utt}) and loads
@{tt}provider.py@{utt} when a model entry names one of its providers. There
is no entry in @{tt}config/plugins.yaml@{utt} and no allowlist line. A
misspelt provider name fails when the configuration loads.

@{table}
Manifest key | Names
provides | openai_httpx, openai_responses
provides_batch | openai_httpx
provides_tts | openai_speech
default_base_url | openai_httpx: https://api.openai.com/v1, openai_responses: https://openrouter.ai/api/v1
@{body}

@{h2}Dependencies

@{tt}httpx@{utt}, which the core requires anyway. No SDK.

@{h2}Where things live

Nothing on disk. Batch input files go where the core's batch queue says.
Changes to @{tt}llm.yaml@{utt} reach an agent when it builds its client,
i.e. after a restart.
@endnode
